    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n28476" root="عذق" body-key="E">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">عَذِقٌ</span></b> , applied to a man, <i>i. q.</i> <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">لَبِقٌ</span>: (O, K:) so
in the phrase <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">عَذِقٌ بِالقُلُوبِ</span> [app. meaning <i>Congenial with hearts</i>]. (O, TA.) ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b2-  Applied to perfume, <i>Fragrant.</i> (O, K.) ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b3-  <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">نَعْجَةٌ عَذِقَةٌ</span> <i>A ewe
having goodly wool:</i> one should not say <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">عَنْزٌ عَذِقَةٌ</span>.
(Ibn-'Abbád, O.)

